Independent review of T1 at `7993590e0575ce8da825cdf7572895cb0a6f15d2`, compared with `refs/heads/redesign` = `94789e6141d9d2213d3b909b07bcbac9bbcdb02d`.

Verdict: fix the host-boundary normalization problem below before merging. One major correctness finding; no demonstrated runtime soundness failure in the accepted probes. No tracked files were edited and no commits were created.

The criteria were written in [criteria.md](criteria.md) before opening the compiler diff, after reading the controlling specification and designs. They require exact finite/cofinite scalar sets; principal schemes without contextual inferred widening; conservative known facts; matching, coverage and runtime tests that agree; correct variance, error/service rows and Assert frames; directional host checks; exact package interfaces; and usable display/performance. This review treats the explicitly documented list/shared-field inference limitation and the amended None-accumulator example as controlling requirements.

**Finding 1 — major: host acceptance depends on syntax rather than the scalar value set. Executed checker reproductions and code inspection.**

```i
One = {1 | 2} - 2
@external("app", "write")
write: (One) -> Nil
```

Expected: accepted, with the same Int ABI as `write: (1) -> Nil`. `One` is exactly `1`, and §10.3 permits any Int value set where i supplies the value.

Actual: E0318 at `src/main.i:3:9`, saying the parameter is not a type the host can take. Replacing `One` by `1` succeeds. `One = Int & 1` is also rejected. Both forms work as ordinary i types.

The incoming direction has the same defect:

```i
Whole = {Int - 0} | 0
@external("app", "read")
read: () -> Whole
```

Expected: accepted, because Whole = Int. Actual: E0318 at `3:13`. The otherwise identical `read: () -> Int` succeeds. This does not ask the host to honor a restricted literal type; the normalized type contains every Int.

A host-service error row also fails to combine scalar members:

```i
@host("app")
Svc { Svc(get: () -> String ? String | "error") }
```

Expected: accepted; its error type is String. Actual: E0320 at `2:16`.

Cause: `resolve.rs:1679`'s `scalar_abi` recognizes a syntax whitelist. Its Union arm accepts only literal/named leaves (`1692–1700`), its Diff arm requires a named whole scalar first (`1710–1717`), and it has no Inter arm. Host-service errors are split into written union parts and checked individually at `1538–1548`. Thus equal types receive different ABI admissibility decisions. Normalize scalar expressions with the same finite/cofinite algebra used by inference before applying the incoming-whole/outgoing-any rule; merge error-row members before checking their sets. Preserve rejection of genuinely restricted incoming values.

Reproduction directories: `host_equivalent_finite`, `host_equivalent_inter`, `host_equivalent_whole`, `host_service_union_error`. Accepted controls: `host_one_control`, `host_whole_control`, `host_union_internal`. Each contains source and checker output in `result.json`. These rejected programs were checked, not executed.

**Hard-case verification.**

| Case | Scratch probe / outcome |
|---|---|
| Arithmetic, concatenation, grouped negative literals, hex and Unicode identity | `operators`: whole arithmetic/string results; canonical singleton annotations accepted; O0/O2 pass |
| Scalar/tuple/nominal accumulators and recursion | `folds`: O0/O2 pass |
| Accumulator containing a function | `functions_acc`: O0/O2 pass |
| Explicit callback input annotations | `typed_function_acc`: O0/O2 pass |
| Generic invariant cells, with/without correct annotation | `invariant`, `annotation_removed`, `annotation_added`: O0/O2 pass; `invariant_bad` rejected |
| Generic variable routing through differences | `generic_difference`: principal-looking `(x: A | 0) -> A - 0`; O0/O2 pass |
| E0520 for direct constants, function results and callback inputs | `equality`, `regress_const`, `regress_result`, `regress_map`: intended E0520 |
| Missing scalar coverage and guards | `missing_int`, `missing_string`, `guards`: E0516 with valid witnesses |
| Finite literals and prefix patterns | `finite`: O0/O2 pass |
| List literal heads | `list_annotated`: O0/O2 pass; `list_inferred`: documented E0516 with annotation hint |
| Shared generic fields | `shared_finite`: all four combinations covered, O0/O2 pass; `shared_inferred`: documented E0516 |
| Partial tuple narrowing | `nested_reject`: correct E0501 |
| Cofinite narrowing and mixed runtime tags | `cofinite`: O0/O2 pass, including boxed positive/negative and very large Ints |
| Prefix/cofinite interaction in a tuple | `prefix_tuple`: O0/O2 pass |
| Deferred Assert in recursive group | `assert_group`: handled through Assert at O0/O2 |
| One unguarded clause with choices | `choices_assert`: reports unmatched 2 through Assert at O0/O2 |
| Literal error rows | `error_rows`: O0/O2 pass |
| Hidden service input and invariant service arguments | `service`: O0/O2 pass; `service_bad`: E0515 |
| Host-provided literal results/errors and inferred export inputs | `host_return`, `host_service`, `host_export_input`: rejected as required |
| Host outbound finite/cofinite types | `host_outbound`: accepted |
| Cross-package exact types beyond hover limit | `cross-lib`, `cross-consumer`: 20 literals preserved in full type output, O0/O2 pass; changing v19 to v99 gives E0520 in consumer |
| Human display | `display_union`: numeric order and 10 displayed + 31 omitted; full `--types` retains all 41 literals |

Eighteen handwritten accepted programs were executed at both optimization levels (36 successful runs). `fuzz.py` additionally generated 180 finite String × Bool matches with literals, prefixes, complement tests, catchalls and False guards. An independent Python membership model enumerated all ten input values. All 180 acceptance decisions agreed; all 65 covering programs ran at both O0/O2 with the expected first-clause result. These are bounded tests, not a proof of soundness or principality.

The release build was confirmed current with the authorized cargo wrapper. `cargo test -p ilang-check --lib --test literal_types --test types --locked` passed 22 library tests, 23 literal tests and 5 type tests; four ignored stress tests were not part of that command. See `check-tests.log`.

**Usability and corpus acceptance.**

The archived baseline compiler SHA-256 is `1008241ef032ec55e5b18816002215ca0e146133e77cf0d04297dbfadd326c4d`, matching the recorded pre-T1 redesign build. The current release hash is `e67efe56ce1afadaf82d35659f9555adfd8a48200d1e479c1374e3b436ed90db`.

All current examples/hello, std and bench projects check with both compilers. Current hello runs at both O0/O2; std reports 1465 passed/0 failed at each level; bench reports 12 passed/0 failed at each level. Logs: `corpus.json`, `runtime-corpus.json`.

The 316-case current-source audit reports 262 kept, 53 added, 0 gained, 1 lost relative to redesign's source inventory. The loss is `crates/ilang-check/tests/types/spec.i:94`, `assert(1 == 2)`, with E0520. See `full-corpus.json`.

That count uses the already-rewritten runtime fixtures. I separately copied the original redesign versions of `tests/spec/11/equality.i`, `tests/spec/11/stop_tail_service.i`, `tests/spec/6/blocks.i`, and `tests/spec/6/strings.i` into scratch projects. All four check with the baseline and fail with T1's specified E0520. Thus the one-loss claim is correct for the current source corpus, but is not the total number of original programs whose acceptance changes. The implementer records these rewrites separately; this is an audit qualification, not an additional correctness finding. See `original-fixtures.json`.

Concrete everyday costs, each reproduced against both compilers:

- Constant equality tests: `x = 1; f = () { x == 2 }` now gives E0520 at the comparison. An equality helper with parameter inputs permits testing runtime equality.
- A literal-returning stub: `status = () { "ok" }; f = () { status() == "error" }` now gives E0520. An explicit String result annotation preserves a broader API for such stubs.
- A callback over fixed data: `list.map(["a"], (x) { x == "b" })` now gives E0520 at `x == "b"`. Writing `(x: String)` permits the reusable comparison. The original stop-tail fixture similarly needs `(v: Int)`.

The finite-list/shared-field annotation requirement is another usability cost, but these unannotated cases were not accepted before T1 either. Ordinary tested accumulators and invariant cell builders did not acquire an annotation requirement. No additional current examples/std/bench acceptance regression was found.

**Golden audit.**

I sampled 77 changed lines across 14 fixtures, reading the source programs and checking the cited rules, including literal propagation, pattern-domain changes, known-type equality, error rows, generic groups, Assert, and complement narrowing. No unjustified semantic change was found in that sample. Exact inventory: `golden-audit.md`. The broader checker golden suite passed without regeneration.

**Scale measurements.**

Wall-clock medians in milliseconds after one warm-up and three measured runs, this environment; not a reserved quiet-window gate. Full samples and baseline comparisons: `stress.json`.

| Shape | 250 | 500 | 1000 | 2000 | 5000 | 10000 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Distinct literal list | 8.8 | 8.7 | 12.7 | 14.2 | 30.2 | 54.8 |
| Scalar clauses + catchall | 8.4 | 11.3 | 21.5 | 47.6 | 210.5 | — |
| Literal alias in 100 functions | 81.8 | 126.6 | 254.8 | 603.6 | — | — |

A 50×50 nested literal match checked in 2569 ms (10×10: 11.1 ms; 20×20: 62.3 ms; 30×30: 319.5 ms). Monomorphic local calls: 2500 in 24.2 ms, 5000 in 38.9 ms. All measured checks terminated successfully. The 1000-item baseline timings were 6.5 ms for the literal list and 103.5 ms for scalar clauses. These results reveal substantial nested-matrix cost but do not establish a new performance regression; I am not filing one.

Required before merge: repair Finding 1 and add equivalent-form boundary tests for both directions and service errors. Keep the genuine restricted-host-input rejections. No runtime soundness blocker was demonstrated by this review.
